data.content:
The lightest approach for modern .NET (8+) is the built-in **`TimeProvider`** class — no DI container needed, just a constructor parameter:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In production** — use the default: `new InvoiceCalculator()`  
**In tests** — pass a fake:

```csharp
// Add NuGet: Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-30)); // travel backwards if needed
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (.NET 8+) — zero new abstractions to write
- The optional parameter keeps the public API non-breaking
- No DI container required; works with plain `new`

